## Why This Is Asked

This question evaluates understanding of end-to-end testing for real-time media systems at scale, with emphasis on synchronization verification, fault injection, and observability under production-like conditions.

## Key Concepts

- Large-scale WebRTC testing with synthetic clients
- Network emulation including latency, jitter, and packet loss profiles
- Audio-video synchronization verification
- Recording integrity and consistency across media servers
- Burst load testing and stress testing methodologies
- Signaling and media path coordination

## Code Example

```javascript
// Pseudocode for WebRTC E2E test harness
class WebRTCE2EHarness {
  constructor(config) {
    this.rooms = [];
    this.networkProfiles = config.networkProfiles;
    this.statsInterval = config.statsIntervalMs || 1000;
  }

  async createRoom(participants, networkProfile) {
    const room = new Room(participants, networkProfile);
    await room.initialize();
    this.rooms.push(room);
    return room;
  }

  async runBurstLoadTest(options) {
    const { roomCount, participantsPerRoom, staggerDelay } = options;
    for (let i = 0; i < roomCount; i++) {
      await this.createRoom(participantsPerRoom, this.getRandomProfile());
      await this.sleep(staggerDelay);
    }
  }

  collectMetrics() {
    return this.rooms.flatMap(room => room.getRTCStats());
  }

  verifyRecordingSync(recording1, recording2) {
    const drift = Math.abs(recording1.timestamp - recording2.timestamp);
    return drift < 50; // 50ms tolerance
  }
}
```

## Why This Is Asked
Streaming UI tests are a common beginner-to-intermediate challenge in E2E testing. This question assesses the ability to design a reproducible test flow that exercises real-time updates, cancel/retry semantics, and test isolation—areas that frequently cause test flakiness in practice.

## Key Concepts
- End-to-end streaming validation (SSE/WebSocket)
- Progressive rendering and ordering of chunks
- Cancel and retry behavior testing
- Test data isolation with sandboxed backends
- Network conditions simulation (throttling, outages)

## Code Example
```javascript
// Playwright test sketch (pseudo-implementation)
test('streaming playground flow', async ({ page }) => {
  // Setup mock backend with isolated test data
  await setupTestBackend({ isolated: true });

  // User interactions
  await page.fill('[data-testid="prompt-input"]', 'Hello world');
  await page.selectOption('[data-testid="model-select"]', 'gpt-4');
  await page.fill('[data-testid="temperature"]', '0.7');

  // Start streaming and verify chunks
  const runButton = page.locator('[data-testid="run-button"]');
  await runButton.click();

  // Verify progressive rendering
  await expect(page.locator('[data-testid="output-pane"]'))
    .toContainText('Hello', { timeout: 1000 });
  await expect(page.locator('[data-testid="output-pane"]'))
    .toContainText('Hello world', { timeout: 2000 });

  // Test cancel behavior
  await page.locator('[data-testid="cancel-button"]').click();
  await expect(page.locator('[data-testid="output-pane"]'))
    .not.toContainText('complete');

  // Test retry with different parameters
  await page.selectOption('[data-testid="model-select"]', 'gpt-3.5');
  await runButton.click();
  await expect(page.locator('[data-testid="output-pane"]'))
    .toContainText('Hello world', { timeout: 3000 });
});
```
